{"reference":"Matthew 6","book":"Matthew","chapter":6,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Public piety and its setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Matthew 6 treats giving, prayer, fasting, possessions, and trust as matters vulnerable to public status performance. Its references to Scripture, Torah debate, religious practices, and institutions place this criticism within diverse Jewish worlds, even though later identity conflict also shapes Matthew's polemic. The chapter's repeated contrast is therefore not simply between religious activity and irreligion, but between being seen by others and seeking the Father who sees in secret.","verse_refs":["Matthew 6:1-18","Matthew 6:1-34"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_006_15f5037d47df0f1f0f9a2b6a"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}